      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says that an accepting bounded tableau “happens exactly when x in L.” The current tableau definition has no accepting-state stuttering convention: def-bounded-computation-tableau requires every later row to arise by a legal transition, while def-dtime-ntime-dspace-and-nspace defines the transition map only on Q minus {q_acc,q_rej}. Take a one-tape machine that accepts input 1 in one step but takes three steps on input 0. Any uniform polynomial time bound for length-one inputs has T at least 3. For x=1, x is in L, but after the accepting row there is no legal transition with which to fill the required remaining rows of the (T+1)x(T+1) tableau. Thus no accepting bounded tableau exists. A self-loop/padding convention or a final-row acceptance condition is required before this inference is valid.",
